> And this is the place where I think it's easy to gauge significance.
> Most games will have 25 spellbooks if you survive to the valley of the

You cannot pull numbers out of thin air when you are dealing with 
statistics and probability; you need to show hard numbers, and you need 
to back it up with cites from the code.  There are only 42 spellbooks in
the game, for example: to say that more than half of them are generated
by the Valley "most" of the time is the kind of extraordinary claim that
demands hard proof.  Spellbooks are not that common an object.

Further, you polypile spellbooks, _guaranteeing_ that your perceptions
will be skewed high -- you may well HAVE seen 25 spellbooks by the castle,
but nowhere near that many people polypile spellbooks, and thus your
perceptions are completely invalid for determining the general case.
(for one, it's not particularly useful for a Valkryie or the like to do
so!)

In this case, the hard proof is that the chance of a purely random
Create Familiar spellbook is 1/100, and of a Charm Monster spellbook is
2/100, for any given randomly generated spellbook.  The math does not
lie; a billion-dollar industry in Las Vegas was built on this fact.  

The difference between 1/100 and 2/100 is negligible when considering
the case of any given randomly generated spellbook.  Swapping the two 
is going to have no noticeable effect on the average player's likelihood 
of finding (and then using!) the spell.

